{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AJjz6bQ7CZhu1KD5YAZfRK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n3\t---\n4\t\n5\t# BUILD & TEST\n6\t\n7\t## Commands\n8\t\n9\t```sh\n10\t# Tests (Babashka runner)\n11\tbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\n12\tbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\n13\tbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n14\t# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\n15\tbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\n16\tbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n17\tbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\n18\tbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\n19\tbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n20\tbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n21\tbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\n22\tbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n23\tbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n24\t# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n25\t# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n26\t# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n27\t# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n28\t# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n29\t# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n30\t# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n31\t# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n32\t# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n33\t# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n34\t# which lanes may overlap.\n35\t#\n36\t# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n37\t# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n38\t# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n39\t# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n40\t# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n41\t# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n42\t# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n43\t# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n44\t# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n45\t# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n46\t# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n47\t# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n48\t# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n49\t# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n50\t# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n51\t# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n52\t# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n53\t# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n54\t# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n55\t# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n56\t# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n57\t# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n58\t# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n59\t# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\n60\tnpm test             # Node.js tests\n61\t\n62\t# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n63\t# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n64\t# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n65\t# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\n66\tbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\n67\tbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n68\t                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n69\t                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n70\t                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\t### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n74\t\n75\t`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\n76\treach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\n77\tmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\n78\ttests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\n79\tA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\n80\tfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\n81\tio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n82\t(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\n83\tlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\n84\tneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n85\t`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\n86\ttypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\n87\tland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\n88\tchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\n89\tantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\n90\tas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\n91\tdao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n92\t(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\n93\tand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n94\t\n95\t### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n96\t\n97\t`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\n98\tone or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\n99\tis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n100\t\n101\t```sh\n102\tbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\n103\tbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\n104\tbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\n105\tbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\n106\tbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\n107\tbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\n108\tbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n109\t```\n110\t\n111\t#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n112\t\n113\t- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n114\t- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n115\t- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n116\t- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n117\t- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n118\t- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n119\t\n120\tPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\n121\tand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\n122\tselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n123\t\n124\t#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n125\t\n126\t1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n127\t2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n128\t3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n129\t4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n130\t5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n131\t\n132\t\n133\tThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\n134\tslice L5) is two tests:\n135\t\n136\t- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n137\t  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n138\t  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n139\t  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n140\t  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n141\t  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n142\t  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n143\t  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n144\t- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n145\t  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n146\t  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n147\t  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n148\t  REPL.\n149\t\n150\t```sh\n151\t# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\n152\tclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\n153\tclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\n154\tclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n155\t\n156\t# ClojureDart\n157\tclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n158\t\n159\t# Linting\n160\tclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n161\t```\n162\t\n163\tThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n164\t[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\n165\tnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\n166\tDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\n167\twith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n168\t`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\n169\texits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\n170\teach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\n171\tscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n172\t<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n173\t(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n174\t`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\n175\tunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\n176\tdifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\n177\tpackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n178\t\n179\t`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\n180\tcompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\n181\taggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\n182\tstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\n183\tbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n184\t\n185\t## Test tiers: quick and slow\n186\t\n187\tThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n188\t(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n189\t\n190\t- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n191\t  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n192\t  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n193\t- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n194\t  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n195\t- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n196\t  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n197\t  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n198\t  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n199\t  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n200\t  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n201\t  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n202\t  with the method below before changing the cut.\n203\t- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n204\t  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n205\t  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n206\t\n207\tMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\n208\tservers\" for how to run them):\n209\t\n210\t| Run | Time |\n211\t|---|---|\n212\t| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n213\t| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n214\t| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n215\t| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n216\t\n217\tThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\n218\tSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n219\t`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\n220\tunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\n221\tA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n222\t\n223\t**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\n224\trun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\n225\totherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n226\t\n227\t```sh\n228\tNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n229\t# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n230\t#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n231\t# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n232\t# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n233\t```\n234\t\n235\t**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\n236\tshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\n237\ttests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\n238\tJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n239\t\n240\t```sh\n241\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n242\t# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\n243\tset -u\n244\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n245\tcd \"$1\" || exit 2\n246\tPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\n247\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n248\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n249\tOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n250\tts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\n251\techo \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n252\tnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n253\tbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n254\tbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n255\tSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n256\t: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n257\tfor s in $SUBS; do\n258\t  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n259\t  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n260\t  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\n261\tdone\n262\t# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\n263\tfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\n264\tsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\n265\tcomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\n266\techo \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n267\trun_shard() {\n268\t  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n269\t  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n270\t  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n271\t  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n272\t}\n273\texport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\n274\tprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\n275\techo \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n276\t```\n277\t\n278\t**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n279\t\n280\t```sh\n281\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n282\t# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\n283\tset -u\n284\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n285\tcd \"$1\" || exit 2\n286\tPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\n287\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n288\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n289\tOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n290\trun_ns() {\n291\t  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n292\t  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n293\t  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n294\t}\n295\texport -f run_ns; export OUT\n296\techo \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n297\txargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\n298\techo \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n299\t```\n300\t\n301\t## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n302\t\n303\tTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\n304\treached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\n305\tand `git`.\n306\t\n307\t| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n308\t|---|---|---|\n309\t| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n310\t| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n311\t\n312\t**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\n313\tthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\n314\towner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n315\t\n316\t### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n317\t\n318\tRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\n319\twould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\n320\tdirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\n321\tbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\n322\tthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n323\t\n324\t```sh\n325\t# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\n326\tssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n327\t  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n328\t  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n329\t  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n330\t  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n331\t```\n332\t\n333\tTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n334\t`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\n335\twas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n336\t\n337\t### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n338\t\n339\tA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\n340\ttools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\n341\tinto the clone:\n342\t\n343\t```sh\n344\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\n345\tcd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\n346\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\n347\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n348\t```\n349\t\n350\tActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n351\t(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\n352\tfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n353\t\n354\t### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n355\t\n356\t\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\n357\tCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n358\t`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\n359\tavoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\n360\tlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n361\t\n362\t- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n363\t  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n364\t  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n365\t  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n366\t  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n367\t  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n368\t  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n369\t  verified by a measured run.\n370\t- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n371\t  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n372\t- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n373\t  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n374\t- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n375\t  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n376\t- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n377\t\n378\tThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\n379\tthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\n380\tseparate Dart clone.\n381\t\n382\t### The full run\n383\t\n384\tRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\n385\tserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\n386\tthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\n387\ttasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\n388\tlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\n389\tsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n390\t\n391\t```sh\n392\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n393\t# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\n394\tset -u\n395\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n396\tDART=\"${2:-}\"\n397\tcd \"$1\" || exit 2\n398\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n399\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n400\tLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\n401\tts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\n402\techo \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n403\tnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n404\tbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n405\tbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n406\tbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\n407\tlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n408\t         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\n409\tlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\n410\tlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\n411\tif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n412\t  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n413\t    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n414\t    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\n415\tfi\n416\twait\n417\techo \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n418\t```\n419\t\n420\t```sh\n421\tssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n422\t```\n423\t\n424\tWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\n425\tpress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\n426\tand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\n427\twrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n428\t`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\n429\tscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n430\t(measured 2026-10-11).\n431\t\n432\t### Known differences from the laptop\n433\t\n434\t- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n435\t  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n436\t  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n437\t  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n438\t  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n439\t  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n440\t- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n441\t  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n442\t- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n443\t  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n444\t\n445\t### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n446\t\n447\tShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\n448\tit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\n449\tNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\n450\tnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\n451\tlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\n452\textra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n453\t\n454\t## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n455\t\n456\tWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n457\t\n458\tTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n459\t\n460\tWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n461\t1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n462\t2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n463\t3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n464\t\n465\tThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n466\t\n467\t## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n468\t\n469\tA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n470\t\n471\t1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n472\t   ```sh\n473\t   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n474\t   ```\n475\t\n476\t2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n477\t   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n478\t\n479\t3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n480\t   Common patterns:\n481\t   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n482\t   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n483\t   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n484\t\n485\t4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n486\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee657710-cc1c-4c43-882b-5874c9a79051","uuid":"bcc3c461-3b67-4b79-a26c-77bde93837df","timestamp":"2026-10-12T15:52:29.254Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6b/docs/agents/build-n-test.md","content":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n","numLines":486,"startLine":1,"totalLines":486}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AJjz6bQ7CZhu1KD5YAZfRK","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HUYRAYZpubbgatxtoyJV2h","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation: phase 6, the continuation format and the row carrier\n2\t\n3\tStatus: **Companion design, phase 6 of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\n4\t§10.** Written 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\n5\tdesign as amended (`8f0b9e38`), the landed 4a evaluator\n6\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`) and the 4b stage-1 evaluator\n7\ton branch `srvm-phase4b` (read only), the merged phase-5 derivations\n8\tdesign, the UCF document and its version-2 amendment, the row-carrier\n9\tdraft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, body version 3, never landed),\n10\tthe two de Bruijn kernels, and the landed UCF code and harness\n11\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`,\n12\t`test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`). Revision 2 (2026-10-11)\n13\tapplies the codex review\n14\t`collab/1791760000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n15\t(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the task's free environment is carried explicitly\n16\t(§4.4, §6.3), the stack kernel's K slices and tail completion are\n17\tcorrected and gated (§4.3, §8.3), register return destinations are\n18\trecovered from A (§4.2), `act` and `tail` have separate algorithms, the\n19\tvalidator has a complete pure input contract and the carrier's order\n20\t(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\n21\ta specified re-emission (§5.5), the harness matrix is expanded (§5.2),\n22\t6a is the independent codec (§8.1), and the amendment inventory is\n23\texhaustive (§10). Revision 3 (2026-10-11) applies the codex round-2\n24\treview\n25\t`collab/1791800000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n26\t(CHANGES_REQUESTED): stack lowering bases are cumulative through each\n27\tslice (§4.3), the live operand subtracts the virtual destination before\n28\tmapping (§4.2, §5.6), the free environment's source on lift is specified\n29\twith its owner (§6.3), E2 requires lexical presence and X1 validates the\n30\troot's free environment (§3.2), the harness oracles of rows 15, 20, 22\n31\tand 23 and the segment-wrapper results are corrected (§5.2), the\n32\tcross-lane re-emission takes an explicit stream correspondence (§5.5),\n33\tand the amendment residue is closed (§10.3 R9, U7). The frozen document\n34\tis not changed by this one.\n35\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), including its\n36\tunitarity invariant, and to\n37\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md)\n38\t(\"the frozen design\"); format and rigor model:\n39\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md)\n40\tand [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md).\n41\t\n42\tEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (an owner ruling, frozen\n43\ttext, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\n44\tdocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n45\t`8f0b9e38`; line numbers are those of that commit; the 4b branch is\n46\tcited as `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc`, with the line numbers of\n47\tits stage-1 commit (`0f4522b9`) unless a citation says stage 2\n48\t(`bdf48cf4`, committed after revision 2; stage 2 moved the reified\n49\tpayload to `:256`, `create-vm` to `:349-375` and `gc-roots`/`gc-children`\n50\tto `:485-488`, and changed none of the shapes this document reads).\n51\t\n52\tContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the canonical\n53\tframe · §2 the row carrier · §3 the frame validator · §4 layouts · §5\n54\tthe safepoint harness · §6 code and task context in the carrier · §7\n55\tlift and lower · §8 slices, gates and phase 7's entry conditions · §9\n56\tverification honesty · §10 findings and amendments · §11 owner\n57\tquestions · §12 portability.\n58\t\n59\t---\n60\t\n61\t## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n62\t\n63\t### 0.1 Rulings that bind this design (DECIDED)\n64\t\n65\t1. **A is the only code identity**; new identities digest under Jing's\n66\t   default (BLAKE3), never SHA-256 (`ucf/code-address` is\n67\t   `jing/segment-key`, `ucf.cljc:247-251`).\n68\t2. **No legacy**: one body version, no reader for older bodies, no\n69\t   compat shim, no migration path; clean breaks.\n70\t3. **Unitarity**: the AST is never lost and is always retrievable\n71\t   wherever code is lowered; no design assumes a tree-less receiver; the\n72\t   segment-to-tree association of the phase-5 design (§9.2 there) is a\n73\t   given, including its completeness rule: an absent entry **or an empty\n74\t   tree set** is incomplete.\n75\t4. **The vector-to-datoms mapping is retired**; the carrier carries\n76\t   positional tuples and rows, never datoms-from-vector.\n77\t5. **dao.stream is the complexity boundary**: no transport mechanics.\n78\t6. **One truth, many interpretations**: the register machine's\n79\t   configuration `C = ⟨seg, pc, W⟩` with `E`, `K` is the canonical frame\n80\t   shape; the stack and register kernels reconstruct it through their\n81\t   layouts (frozen §4.3, §10 phase 6).\n82\t7. **No file of phase 4b or phase 5 is touched**, so this phase runs\n83\t   concurrently with them (§8.5 names the dependencies that remain, and\n84\t   the one old-kernel file 6c must change).\n85\t\n86\t### 0.2 The answer\n87\t\n88\tA continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register\n89\tmachine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return`\n90\tframes, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait\n91\t(frozen §2.4, §4.2), plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\n92\tenvironment**, store slice, module stores, parked table, cursor cells, code by\n93\tA and the tree association. **§1** restates the frame as the 4b\n94\tevaluator mints it. **§2** gives the row carrier: body version 3, one\n95\tprofile (`\"v4\"`), Merkle content rows under Jing's default digest, the\n96\twindow as a sorted keyed row in which a nil value is a present\n97\t`[:lit nil]`, `:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\n98\t`:parked-table` rows replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n99\t`free-env` slot, the pending rows and census unchanged. **§3** gives the\n100\tvalidator as an ordered set of rules over the decoded frame, the carried\n101\tcode and decoded task context, engine-independent, run after canonical\n102\tnumbering as the carrier orders it. **§4** defines the two layouts with\n103\tseparate `act` and `tail` algorithms: the register kernel's physical\n104\tregisters are paired with A's virtual ones pc by pc and its return\n105\tdestinations recovered from A's call; the stack kernel's operand stack\n106\tis sliced `[previous-base, base_i)` per frame, and its tail completion is\n107\ta kernel seam this phase must add and gate. **§5** is the harness over\n108\tthree lanes with an expanded, concrete matrix and a specified\n109\tre-emission procedure for cross-lane equality. **§6** fixes what travels\n110\tfor code and task context. **§8** cuts the slices: 6a the independent\n111\tframe and row codec, 6b full task transport (needs phase 5's scanners\n112\tand 4b stage 2), 6c the layouts and the stack tail-completion seam\n113\t(needs 5a/5b).\n114\t\n115\t---\n116\t\n117\t## 1. The canonical frame\n118\t\n119\t### 1.1 Shapes, as the evaluator mints them\n120\t\n121\t**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4 \"Delivery\", §4.2; evaluator design §1.3-1.6;\n122\t`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:61-84` for `act-payload`,\n123\t`entry-builders`, `tail-builders`; `:229-234` for the reified payload\n124\tand the park). Two closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n125\t\n126\t```clojure\n127\tact  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd r}}\n128\ttail = {:segment seg :site n :k K :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n129\tret  = {:type :return :segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :rd r}     ; one frame kind of K\n130\t```\n131\t\n132\t- `:pc` of an `act` is the resume pc, already `pc+1` of the parking\n133\t  instruction; `:site` of a `tail` is the tail call's own pc (evaluator\n134\t  §1.5).\n135\t- `W` of an `act` is `saved(pc, rd) = W↾(L(pc) − {rd})`\n136\t  (`analysis/saved`, `analysis.cljc:92-97`), built with `select-keys`,\n137\t  so a nil-valued live register is a present key.\n138\t- `K` is a vector of `ret` frames, innermost last, or nil when empty\n139\t  (`put-registers`, `semantic_register.cljc:47-52`); each frame's window\n140\t  is `saved(frame.pc, frame.rd)` of its own body.\n141\t- A **parked record** (`:park`, and FFI bookkeeping under a call id) is\n142\t  an `act` payload plus `:type :parked-continuation :id`\n143\t  (`engine/park-continuation`; evaluator §1.6); the FFI bookkeeping\n144\t  record of an effectful **tail** call is a `tail` payload (evaluator\n145\t  §1.6).\n146\t- A **reified continuation** is `values/continuation owner (assoc act\n147\t  :type :reified-continuation)` (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:229-232`).\n148\t- The wait entry the engine holds is the `act` or `tail` payload plus\n149\t  the engine's fields (`:reason`, `:cursor-ref`, `:stream-id`, `:datom`,\n150\t  `:call-id`, `:request-sent`, the link fields), which travel as the\n151\t  **pending** (§2.4), never as frame slots.\n152\t- The task's **free environment** is the `:env` a `create-vm` received\n153\t  (`semantic_register.cljc:261`): every activation's `E` is that map\n154\t  merged under the lexical chain's bindings (§4.4), and a de Bruijn\n155\t  kernel holds the same map as its task-wide `:free-env`\n156\t  (`debruijn/stack.cljc:82, 418, 683`; `debruijn/register.cljc:50`).\n157\t\n158\t### 1.2 The UCF frame, keyed form\n159\t\n160\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2), restated with one naming decision (§10.1 F1):\n161\t\n162\t```clojure\n163\t;; act state\n164\t{:yin.k/state :act\n165\t :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n\n166\t :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n167\t :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly saved(pc, rd); sorted by r on the wire\n168\t :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n169\t :yin.k/k [{:yin.k/frame-type :return :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n170\t            :yin.k/env E :yin.k/window {r V …} :yin.k/rd r} …]\n171\t :yin.k/pending {…}}                        ; absent on a parked record and a reified value\n172\t\n173\t;; tail state\n174\t{:yin.k/state :tail\n175\t :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/site n\n176\t :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n177\t :yin.k/k [ … ]\n178\t :yin.k/pending {…}}\n179\t```\n180\t\n181\tThis keyed form is the **decoded canonical frame**: what the validator\n182\tof §3 judges, what the layouts of §4 produce and consume, and what the\n183\tharness compares across lanes. The rows of §2 are its carrier and the\n184\ttwo are in bijection (§2.6).\n185\t\n186\t---\n187\t\n188\t## 2. The row carrier\n189\t\n190\tThe row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`) is adopted\n191\t**except** where it describes the stack-shaped machine or legacy\n192\treaders; §10.3 lists every replaced or deleted clause. Its §3 (rows, id\n193\tspaces, census, the explicit \"version 3 verifies under BLAKE3\n194\tpermanently\" rule), §4 (value union), §5.1 (value rows, except\n195\t`:reified`), §5.3 (pending), §5.4 (task context, except\n196\t`:parked-table`), §6 (canonical cursor-cell numbering), §7 (equality) and §8\n197\tsteps 1-6 and 8-12 stand.\n198\t\n199\t### 2.1 Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n200\t\n201\t**DECIDED** by ruling 2 and ruling 6. The carrier is **body version 3**.\n202\tVersions 0-2 are the old handoff's; the row reader refuses them at the\n203\tgate (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) and never reads, normalises or upgrades\n204\tone. Their reader (`yin.vm.ucf.handoff`) is untouched by this phase and\n205\tleaves with the stack-shaped machine at phase 8; that coexistence is\n206\tsequencing, not permission for the row reader to accept a legacy body.\n207\tThe wire unit is the draft's:\n208\t\n209\t```clojure\n210\t{:yin.k/handoff true  :yin.k/version 3  :yin.k/root T  :yin.k/rows [[id kind & slots] …]}\n211\t```\n212\t\n213\t**PROPOSED** (reviewer: ACCEPTED). The profile map is\n214\t`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}` and nothing else; there is\n215\t**no `:yin.k/engine` key**. The frame is the canonical machine's and\n216\tengine-independent by ruling 6; the kernel that executes it is the\n217\treceiving composition's choice. The version-2 registry\n218\t(`ucf/profiles`, `ucf.cljc:51-74`) is not consulted.\n219\t\n220\t### 2.2 Rows, ids, census\n221\t\n222\t**DECIDED** (draft §3, ruling 1). Every row is `[id kind & slots]`;\n223\tcontent rows have `id = (jing/segment-key body)` under Jing's default\n224\tdigest, BLAKE3 for version 3 permanently; identity rows are cursor cells\n225\t`:yin.k/cc-<n>` numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n226\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`); the root carries\n227\tthe cursor-cell census. (The words cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell\n228\tare used exactly as defined in UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor, cursor\n229\tcell, cursor-ref, cell\". Before 2026-10-12 the cursor cells were called\n230\t\"cells\" and spelled `:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:cells`, `:cell-body`; the version-2\n231\thandoff carrier keeps those spellings.) One ordering rule: \"sorted\" is by the Jing canonical\n232\tbytes of the key as spelled.\n233\t\n234\t### 2.3 The frame rows (replacing draft §5.2)\n235\t\n236\t**DECIDED** by frozen §4.2's last paragraph; **PROPOSED** names `:act`\n237\tand `:tail` (§10.1 F1, reviewer: ACCEPTED):\n238\t\n239\t| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n240\t|---|---|---|\n241\t| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n242\t| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n243\t| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n244\t| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n245\t| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n246\t| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n247\t| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n248\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n249\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (draft §5.1) |\n250\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n251\t\n252\t`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\n253\tstructural rows, never a `V`; the draft's \"a reference-free collection\n254\tmust be `[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is\n255\tredundant with the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (rule A2), as\n256\t`pc` is redundant with the pending and checked.\n257\t\n258\t### 2.4 Pending rows\n259\t\n260\t**DECIDED** (draft §5.3, UCF §7.4.3, v2 amendment §2): one kind\n261\t`:pending`; reasons `:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request\n262\t:link-response :install` with the draft's slots, unchanged. What\n263\tchanges is only which state a pending rides on: a `:wait` pairs a\n264\tpending with an `:act` (write-result) **or a `:tail`** (through-return),\n265\tthe frozen §4.1 table's two state columns. The 4b evaluator's in-VM\n266\tmarkers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n267\t`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`) are the pending's\n268\t(`handoff.cljc:702-724`, `:3219-3229`).\n269\t\n270\t### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n271\t\n272\t**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n273\t\n274\t- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n275\t  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n276\t  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n277\t  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n278\t- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n279\t  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n280\t  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n281\t  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n282\t  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n283\t  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n284\t  amendment §7.3, kept).\n285\t- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n286\t  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n287\t  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n288\t- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n289\t  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n290\t\n291\t### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n292\t\n293\t**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n294\t\n295\t```clojure\n296\t[T :task\n297\t   version          ; 3\n298\t   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n299\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n300\t   role             ; :root | :child\n301\t   id-counter       ; N\n302\t   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n303\t   store-cref\n304\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n305\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n306\t   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n307\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n308\t   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n309\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n310\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n311\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n312\t   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n313\t   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n314\t```\n315\t\n316\tNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\n317\tprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\n318\tstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n319\t\n320\t### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n321\t\n322\t**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\n323\tare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n324\t`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\n325\tkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n326\t`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n327\t`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\n328\tvalidator decides.\n329\t\n330\t---\n331\t\n332\t## 3. The frame validator\n333\t\n334\t### 3.1 The pure input contract\n335\t\n336\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2: membership recomputed from A; representation,\n337\tbody scope, site/pc pairing, destination equality, delivery mode,\n338\treturn-frame sites, `K` structure, pending compatibility). **PROPOSED**\n339\t(reviewer: publish the complete contract):\n340\t\n341\t```clojure\n342\t(frame/defect frame code context task)\n343\t;; frame   : a decoded canonical frame (§1.2), any shape (the rules judge it)\n344\t;; code    : {A vector}, every vector already code/well-formed? (step 4 of the carrier ran)\n345\t;; context : :wait | :captured | :parked\n346\t;; task    : decoded task context, plain data, no engine:\n347\t;;           {:parked-key P-or-nil                 ; the parked-table key this frame sits under (:parked only)\n348\t;;            :ffi-call-ids #{…}                   ; call ids of the task's live :ffi and :ffi-request pendings\n349\t;;            :free-env {sym V …}}                 ; the root's free environment, decoded\n350\t;; => nil, or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n351\t\n352\t(frame/task-defect task)\n353\t;; the once-per-body rule X1 over the decoded task context (its :free-env);\n354\t;; run before any frame of the body; => nil or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n355\t```\n356\t\n357\tNamespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame`, file\n358\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc`, requiring only\n359\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `yin.vm.debruijn`\n360\t(`resolve-name`, for the chain of rule E2) and `yin.vm`\n361\t(`reserved-name?`). It knows no kernel and reads no engine: `task` is\n362\tdecoded from the rows (the parked table's key, the pendings' call ids,\n363\tthe root's `free-env`), never from a running machine. A frame a stack\n364\tkernel lifted and one the register evaluator lifted are judged by the\n365\tsame function on the same inputs.\n366\t\n367\t### 3.2 The rules\n368\t\n369\tRules run in the order listed; the first defect wins; every defect\n370\tcarries `:yin.k/kind` and `:yin.k/path`. **Structural rules S1-S4 run\n371\tbefore any dependent read**, so later rules may index freely. `a =\n372\t(analysis/analyze v)` per segment; `body(p) = (code/body-index\n373\t(code/body-ranges v) p)`; `k(body)` the body's register count\n374\t(`code/parse`); `inst(p) = (nth v p)`; `chain(b)` the owner chain of\n375\tbody `b` as phase-5 §1.2 (closure pcs innermost first) and `params(c) =\n376\t(nth (nth v c) 2)`.\n377\t\n378\t**Structure (every frame).**\n379\t\n380\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n381\t|---|---|---|\n382\t| S1 | `frame` is a map; `:yin.k/state` is `:act` or `:tail`; an `act` has exactly the keys `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending`; a `tail` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/site :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending` | `:frame-shape` |\n383\t| S2 | `:yin.k/k` is a vector; every element is a map with exactly `:yin.k/frame-type :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/rd`, `:yin.k/frame-type` is `:return` | `:k-shape` (path `[:yin.k/k i]`) |\n384\t| S3 | every `:yin.k/window` present (the act's, each return frame's) is a map whose keys are exact non-negative integers (on the wire: sorted ascending, unique) and whose values are `V` | `:window-representation` |\n385\t| S4 | every `:yin.k/env` present is `{:yin.k/bindings {S V …}}` optionally with `:yin.k/store-of M`; `:yin.k/deliver` is a map | `:env-shape` / `:deliver-shape` |\n386\t\n387\t**Code (every frame).**\n388\t\n389\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n390\t|---|---|---|\n391\t| C1 | `:yin.k/segment` of the frame and of every return frame names a key of `code` | `:segment` |\n392\t| C2 | an `act`'s `pc`, every return frame's `pc` and a `tail`'s `site` are **exact non-negative integers** (`cbor/numeric-kind` `:integer` for a wire value; never a float carrier), checked **before** any indexing; then `0 < pc < (count v)` and `0 ≤ site < (count v)` | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n393\t| C3 | `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame (no body ends in a parking instruction, frozen §3.4 rule 6) | `:pc-body` |\n394\t\n395\t**The current activation (`act` only; a `tail` skips A1-A6).**\n396\t\n397\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n398\t|---|---|---|\n399\t| A1 | `inst(pc−1)` is one of `:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation`, or a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:site` |\n400\t| A2 | `(:yin.k/rd deliver) = (code/rd inst(pc−1))` and `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}` exactly | `:destination` / `:deliver` |\n401\t| A3 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n402\t| A4 | `rd ∉ (keys window)` | `:window-self` |\n403\t| A5 | `(set (keys window)) ⊆ (analysis/saved a pc rd)` | `:window-extra` |\n404\t| A6 | `(analysis/saved a pc rd) ⊆ (set (keys window))` | `:window-missing` |\n405\t\n406\t**The tail site (`tail` only).**\n407\t\n408\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n409\t|---|---|---|\n410\t| T1 | `inst(site)` is a `:call` with `tail?` true; `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :return}` exactly | `:site` / `:deliver` |\n411\t\n412\t**Return frames (every frame, each `K[i]`).**\n413\t\n414\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n415\t|---|---|---|\n416\t| K1 | `inst(frame.pc−1)` in `frame.segment` is a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:return-site` |\n417\t| K2 | `frame.rd = (code/rd inst(frame.pc−1))` | `:return-destination` |\n418\t| K3 | every key of `frame.window` `< k(body(frame.pc))`; `frame.rd ∉ keys`; `(set (keys frame.window)) = (analysis/saved aₛ frame.pc frame.rd)` in that segment's analysis, reported as `:return-window-scope` / `:return-window-self` / `:return-window-extra` / `:return-window-missing` | as named |\n419\t\n420\t**Environments (every `E`: the act's and each return frame's).**\n421\t\n422\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n423\t|---|---|---|\n424\t| E1 | no binding key is reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`) | `:reserved-name` |\n425\t| E2 | **`E` is the free environment under the lexical chain.** With `C = (set (mapcat params (chain (body pc))))` the lexically bound names at that pc (a return frame's chain is that of its own `pc`): (a) **every name of `C` is a key of `bindings`**, a nil binding being a present key (`bind-params` nil-fills, so a lexical name is never absent; an absent one would be nil-filled on lower and break the canonical round trip); (b) `(apply dissoc bindings C) = (apply dissoc (:free-env task) C)` as maps of `V`: a key outside `C` the free environment lacks, a free key `E` lacks, or a differing value, is a defect | `:free-env-mismatch` |\n426\t\n427\t**Task (once per body, before any frame; `frame/task-defect`).**\n428\t\n429\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n430\t|---|---|---|\n431\t| X1 | the root's `free-env` decodes to a map `{S V …}` with symbol keys, sorted and unique on the wire, none reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`, the same check `create-vm` runs on its `:env`, `vm/check-bindings!`, `vm.cljc:161-168`); it is the `:free-env` every E2 compares against | `:free-env-shape` / `:reserved-name` (path `[:free-env]`) |\n432\t\n433\t**Pending and contexts.**\n434\t\n435\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n436\t|---|---|---|\n437\t| P1 | `context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured` and `:parked` require it absent | `:pending-presence` |\n438\t| P2 | (`:wait`) the pending's reason is admitted by `inst(pc−1)` (act) or `inst(site)` (tail): `:stream-next → :next`; `:stream-put → :put`; `:ffi-call → :ffi, :ffi-request`; `:call → :next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response :install`; `:park` and `:current-continuation` admit none (frozen §4.1; `wire-of-kind`, `handoff.cljc:491-504`; ucf-revisions §8) | `:reason-mismatch` |\n439\t| P3 | (`:captured`) the frame is an `act` and `inst(pc−1)` is `:current-continuation` | `:captured-site` |\n440\t| P4 | (`:parked`) either `inst(pc−1)` is `:park` (an `act`), or `(:parked-key task) ∈ (:ffi-call-ids task)` and the frame is an `act` with `inst(pc−1) = :ffi-call` or a `tail` whose `inst(site)` is a tail `:call` (bookkeeping, evaluator §1.6) | `:parked-site` |\n441\t\n442\tRule E2 is the correction the review required (§4.4): a merged `E`\n443\tcannot be split into free and lexical parts without the task's free\n444\tenvironment, so the carrier carries it and the validator checks that\n445\tevery `E` agrees with it off the lexical names. Nothing here reads a\n446\tstack depth, a stack base or a `live` vector.\n447\t\n448\t### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n449\t\n450\t**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\n451\treview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\n452\thashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\n453\tpending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\n454\tcomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\n455\tcontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n456\t`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\n457\tjudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\n458\tis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\n459\tfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n460\t(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\n461\tand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\n462\tover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\n463\tevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n464\t`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\n465\treconstructs.\n466\t\n467\t---\n468\t\n469\t## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n470\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee657710-cc1c-4c43-882b-5874c9a79051","uuid":"c47dc461-de01-46bc-a2a0-7fce764e074b","timestamp":"2026-10-12T15:52:29.543Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6b/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"# yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation: phase 6, the continuation format and the row carrier\n\nStatus: **Companion design, phase 6 of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\n§10.** Written 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\ndesign as amended (`8f0b9e38`), the landed 4a evaluator\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`) and the 4b stage-1 evaluator\non branch `srvm-phase4b` (read only), the merged phase-5 derivations\ndesign, the UCF document and its version-2 amendment, the row-carrier\ndraft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, body version 3, never landed),\nthe two de Bruijn kernels, and the landed UCF code and harness\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`,\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`). Revision 2 (2026-10-11)\napplies the codex review\n`collab/1791760000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the task's free environment is carried explicitly\n(§4.4, §6.3), the stack kernel's K slices and tail completion are\ncorrected and gated (§4.3, §8.3), register return destinations are\nrecovered from A (§4.2), `act` and `tail` have separate algorithms, the\nvalidator has a complete pure input contract and the carrier's order\n(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\na specified re-emission (§5.5), the harness matrix is expanded (§5.2),\n6a is the independent codec (§8.1), and the amendment inventory is\nexhaustive (§10). Revision 3 (2026-10-11) applies the codex round-2\nreview\n`collab/1791800000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): stack lowering bases are cumulative through each\nslice (§4.3), the live operand subtracts the virtual destination before\nmapping (§4.2, §5.6), the free environment's source on lift is specified\nwith its owner (§6.3), E2 requires lexical presence and X1 validates the\nroot's free environment (§3.2), the harness oracles of rows 15, 20, 22\nand 23 and the segment-wrapper results are corrected (§5.2), the\ncross-lane re-emission takes an explicit stream correspondence (§5.5),\nand the amendment residue is closed (§10.3 R9, U7). The frozen document\nis not changed by this one.\nSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), including its\nunitarity invariant, and to\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md)\n(\"the frozen design\"); format and rigor model:\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md)\nand [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md).\n\nEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (an owner ruling, frozen\ntext, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\ndocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n`8f0b9e38`; line numbers are those of that commit; the 4b branch is\ncited as `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc`, with the line numbers of\nits stage-1 commit (`0f4522b9`) unless a citation says stage 2\n(`bdf48cf4`, committed after revision 2; stage 2 moved the reified\npayload to `:256`, `create-vm` to `:349-375` and `gc-roots`/`gc-children`\nto `:485-488`, and changed none of the shapes this document reads).\n\nContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the canonical\nframe · §2 the row carrier · §3 the frame validator · §4 layouts · §5\nthe safepoint harness · §6 code and task context in the carrier · §7\nlift and lower · §8 slices, gates and phase 7's entry conditions · §9\nverification honesty · §10 findings and amendments · §11 owner\nquestions · §12 portability.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n\n### 0.1 Rulings that bind this design (DECIDED)\n\n1. **A is the only code identity**; new identities digest under Jing's\n   default (BLAKE3), never SHA-256 (`ucf/code-address` is\n   `jing/segment-key`, `ucf.cljc:247-251`).\n2. **No legacy**: one body version, no reader for older bodies, no\n   compat shim, no migration path; clean breaks.\n3. **Unitarity**: the AST is never lost and is always retrievable\n   wherever code is lowered; no design assumes a tree-less receiver; the\n   segment-to-tree association of the phase-5 design (§9.2 there) is a\n   given, including its completeness rule: an absent entry **or an empty\n   tree set** is incomplete.\n4. **The vector-to-datoms mapping is retired**; the carrier carries\n   positional tuples and rows, never datoms-from-vector.\n5. **dao.stream is the complexity boundary**: no transport mechanics.\n6. **One truth, many interpretations**: the register machine's\n   configuration `C = ⟨seg, pc, W⟩` with `E`, `K` is the canonical frame\n   shape; the stack and register kernels reconstruct it through their\n   layouts (frozen §4.3, §10 phase 6).\n7. **No file of phase 4b or phase 5 is touched**, so this phase runs\n   concurrently with them (§8.5 names the dependencies that remain, and\n   the one old-kernel file 6c must change).\n\n### 0.2 The answer\n\nA continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register\nmachine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return`\nframes, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait\n(frozen §2.4, §4.2), plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\nenvironment**, store slice, module stores, parked table, cursor cells, code by\nA and the tree association. **§1** restates the frame as the 4b\nevaluator mints it. **§2** gives the row carrier: body version 3, one\nprofile (`\"v4\"`), Merkle content rows under Jing's default digest, the\nwindow as a sorted keyed row in which a nil value is a present\n`[:lit nil]`, `:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\n`:parked-table` rows replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n`free-env` slot, the pending rows and census unchanged. **§3** gives the\nvalidator as an ordered set of rules over the decoded frame, the carried\ncode and decoded task context, engine-independent, run after canonical\nnumbering as the carrier orders it. **§4** defines the two layouts with\nseparate `act` and `tail` algorithms: the register kernel's physical\nregisters are paired with A's virtual ones pc by pc and its return\ndestinations recovered from A's call; the stack kernel's operand stack\nis sliced `[previous-base, base_i)` per frame, and its tail completion is\na kernel seam this phase must add and gate. **§5** is the harness over\nthree lanes with an expanded, concrete matrix and a specified\nre-emission procedure for cross-lane equality. **§6** fixes what travels\nfor code and task context. **§8** cuts the slices: 6a the independent\nframe and row codec, 6b full task transport (needs phase 5's scanners\nand 4b stage 2), 6c the layouts and the stack tail-completion seam\n(needs 5a/5b).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The canonical frame\n\n### 1.1 Shapes, as the evaluator mints them\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4 \"Delivery\", §4.2; evaluator design §1.3-1.6;\n`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:61-84` for `act-payload`,\n`entry-builders`, `tail-builders`; `:229-234` for the reified payload\nand the park). Two closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n\n```clojure\nact  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd r}}\ntail = {:segment seg :site n :k K :deliver {:deliver :return}}\nret  = {:type :return :segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :rd r}     ; one frame kind of K\n```\n\n- `:pc` of an `act` is the resume pc, already `pc+1` of the parking\n  instruction; `:site` of a `tail` is the tail call's own pc (evaluator\n  §1.5).\n- `W` of an `act` is `saved(pc, rd) = W↾(L(pc) − {rd})`\n  (`analysis/saved`, `analysis.cljc:92-97`), built with `select-keys`,\n  so a nil-valued live register is a present key.\n- `K` is a vector of `ret` frames, innermost last, or nil when empty\n  (`put-registers`, `semantic_register.cljc:47-52`); each frame's window\n  is `saved(frame.pc, frame.rd)` of its own body.\n- A **parked record** (`:park`, and FFI bookkeeping under a call id) is\n  an `act` payload plus `:type :parked-continuation :id`\n  (`engine/park-continuation`; evaluator §1.6); the FFI bookkeeping\n  record of an effectful **tail** call is a `tail` payload (evaluator\n  §1.6).\n- A **reified continuation** is `values/continuation owner (assoc act\n  :type :reified-continuation)` (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:229-232`).\n- The wait entry the engine holds is the `act` or `tail` payload plus\n  the engine's fields (`:reason`, `:cursor-ref`, `:stream-id`, `:datom`,\n  `:call-id`, `:request-sent`, the link fields), which travel as the\n  **pending** (§2.4), never as frame slots.\n- The task's **free environment** is the `:env` a `create-vm` received\n  (`semantic_register.cljc:261`): every activation's `E` is that map\n  merged under the lexical chain's bindings (§4.4), and a de Bruijn\n  kernel holds the same map as its task-wide `:free-env`\n  (`debruijn/stack.cljc:82, 418, 683`; `debruijn/register.cljc:50`).\n\n### 1.2 The UCF frame, keyed form\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2), restated with one naming decision (§10.1 F1):\n\n```clojure\n;; act state\n{:yin.k/state :act\n :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n\n :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly saved(pc, rd); sorted by r on the wire\n :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n :yin.k/k [{:yin.k/frame-type :return :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n            :yin.k/env E :yin.k/window {r V …} :yin.k/rd r} …]\n :yin.k/pending {…}}                        ; absent on a parked record and a reified value\n\n;; tail state\n{:yin.k/state :tail\n :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/site n\n :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n :yin.k/k [ … ]\n :yin.k/pending {…}}\n```\n\nThis keyed form is the **decoded canonical frame**: what the validator\nof §3 judges, what the layouts of §4 produce and consume, and what the\nharness compares across lanes. The rows of §2 are its carrier and the\ntwo are in bijection (§2.6).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The row carrier\n\nThe row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`) is adopted\n**except** where it describes the stack-shaped machine or legacy\nreaders; §10.3 lists every replaced or deleted clause. Its §3 (rows, id\nspaces, census, the explicit \"version 3 verifies under BLAKE3\npermanently\" rule), §4 (value union), §5.1 (value rows, except\n`:reified`), §5.3 (pending), §5.4 (task context, except\n`:parked-table`), §6 (canonical cursor-cell numbering), §7 (equality) and §8\nsteps 1-6 and 8-12 stand.\n\n### 2.1 Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n\n**DECIDED** by ruling 2 and ruling 6. The carrier is **body version 3**.\nVersions 0-2 are the old handoff's; the row reader refuses them at the\ngate (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) and never reads, normalises or upgrades\none. Their reader (`yin.vm.ucf.handoff`) is untouched by this phase and\nleaves with the stack-shaped machine at phase 8; that coexistence is\nsequencing, not permission for the row reader to accept a legacy body.\nThe wire unit is the draft's:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/handoff true  :yin.k/version 3  :yin.k/root T  :yin.k/rows [[id kind & slots] …]}\n```\n\n**PROPOSED** (reviewer: ACCEPTED). The profile map is\n`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}` and nothing else; there is\n**no `:yin.k/engine` key**. The frame is the canonical machine's and\nengine-independent by ruling 6; the kernel that executes it is the\nreceiving composition's choice. The version-2 registry\n(`ucf/profiles`, `ucf.cljc:51-74`) is not consulted.\n\n### 2.2 Rows, ids, census\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §3, ruling 1). Every row is `[id kind & slots]`;\ncontent rows have `id = (jing/segment-key body)` under Jing's default\ndigest, BLAKE3 for version 3 permanently; identity rows are cursor cells\n`:yin.k/cc-<n>` numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`); the root carries\nthe cursor-cell census. (The words cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell\nare used exactly as defined in UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor, cursor\ncell, cursor-ref, cell\". Before 2026-10-12 the cursor cells were called\n\"cells\" and spelled `:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:cells`, `:cell-body`; the version-2\nhandoff carrier keeps those spellings.) One ordering rule: \"sorted\" is by the Jing canonical\nbytes of the key as spelled.\n\n### 2.3 The frame rows (replacing draft §5.2)\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §4.2's last paragraph; **PROPOSED** names `:act`\nand `:tail` (§10.1 F1, reviewer: ACCEPTED):\n\n| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (draft §5.1) |\n| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n\n`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\nstructural rows, never a `V`; the draft's \"a reference-free collection\nmust be `[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is\nredundant with the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (rule A2), as\n`pc` is redundant with the pending and checked.\n\n### 2.4 Pending rows\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §5.3, UCF §7.4.3, v2 amendment §2): one kind\n`:pending`; reasons `:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request\n:link-response :install` with the draft's slots, unchanged. What\nchanges is only which state a pending rides on: a `:wait` pairs a\npending with an `:act` (write-result) **or a `:tail`** (through-return),\nthe frozen §4.1 table's two state columns. The 4b evaluator's in-VM\nmarkers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`) are the pending's\n(`handoff.cljc:702-724`, `:3219-3229`).\n\n### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n\n- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n  amendment §7.3, kept).\n- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n\n### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n\n**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3\n   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N\n   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n```\n\nNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\nprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\nstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n\n### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n\n**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\nare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\nkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\nvalidator decides.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The frame validator\n\n### 3.1 The pure input contract\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2: membership recomputed from A; representation,\nbody scope, site/pc pairing, destination equality, delivery mode,\nreturn-frame sites, `K` structure, pending compatibility). **PROPOSED**\n(reviewer: publish the complete contract):\n\n```clojure\n(frame/defect frame code context task)\n;; frame   : a decoded canonical frame (§1.2), any shape (the rules judge it)\n;; code    : {A vector}, every vector already code/well-formed? (step 4 of the carrier ran)\n;; context : :wait | :captured | :parked\n;; task    : decoded task context, plain data, no engine:\n;;           {:parked-key P-or-nil                 ; the parked-table key this frame sits under (:parked only)\n;;            :ffi-call-ids #{…}                   ; call ids of the task's live :ffi and :ffi-request pendings\n;;            :free-env {sym V …}}                 ; the root's free environment, decoded\n;; => nil, or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n\n(frame/task-defect task)\n;; the once-per-body rule X1 over the decoded task context (its :free-env);\n;; run before any frame of the body; => nil or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n```\n\nNamespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc`, requiring only\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `yin.vm.debruijn`\n(`resolve-name`, for the chain of rule E2) and `yin.vm`\n(`reserved-name?`). It knows no kernel and reads no engine: `task` is\ndecoded from the rows (the parked table's key, the pendings' call ids,\nthe root's `free-env`), never from a running machine. A frame a stack\nkernel lifted and one the register evaluator lifted are judged by the\nsame function on the same inputs.\n\n### 3.2 The rules\n\nRules run in the order listed; the first defect wins; every defect\ncarries `:yin.k/kind` and `:yin.k/path`. **Structural rules S1-S4 run\nbefore any dependent read**, so later rules may index freely. `a =\n(analysis/analyze v)` per segment; `body(p) = (code/body-index\n(code/body-ranges v) p)`; `k(body)` the body's register count\n(`code/parse`); `inst(p) = (nth v p)`; `chain(b)` the owner chain of\nbody `b` as phase-5 §1.2 (closure pcs innermost first) and `params(c) =\n(nth (nth v c) 2)`.\n\n**Structure (every frame).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | `frame` is a map; `:yin.k/state` is `:act` or `:tail`; an `act` has exactly the keys `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending`; a `tail` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/site :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending` | `:frame-shape` |\n| S2 | `:yin.k/k` is a vector; every element is a map with exactly `:yin.k/frame-type :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/rd`, `:yin.k/frame-type` is `:return` | `:k-shape` (path `[:yin.k/k i]`) |\n| S3 | every `:yin.k/window` present (the act's, each return frame's) is a map whose keys are exact non-negative integers (on the wire: sorted ascending, unique) and whose values are `V` | `:window-representation` |\n| S4 | every `:yin.k/env` present is `{:yin.k/bindings {S V …}}` optionally with `:yin.k/store-of M`; `:yin.k/deliver` is a map | `:env-shape` / `:deliver-shape` |\n\n**Code (every frame).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | `:yin.k/segment` of the frame and of every return frame names a key of `code` | `:segment` |\n| C2 | an `act`'s `pc`, every return frame's `pc` and a `tail`'s `site` are **exact non-negative integers** (`cbor/numeric-kind` `:integer` for a wire value; never a float carrier), checked **before** any indexing; then `0 < pc < (count v)` and `0 ≤ site < (count v)` | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n| C3 | `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame (no body ends in a parking instruction, frozen §3.4 rule 6) | `:pc-body` |\n\n**The current activation (`act` only; a `tail` skips A1-A6).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 | `inst(pc−1)` is one of `:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation`, or a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:site` |\n| A2 | `(:yin.k/rd deliver) = (code/rd inst(pc−1))` and `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}` exactly | `:destination` / `:deliver` |\n| A3 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n| A4 | `rd ∉ (keys window)` | `:window-self` |\n| A5 | `(set (keys window)) ⊆ (analysis/saved a pc rd)` | `:window-extra` |\n| A6 | `(analysis/saved a pc rd) ⊆ (set (keys window))` | `:window-missing` |\n\n**The tail site (`tail` only).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 | `inst(site)` is a `:call` with `tail?` true; `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :return}` exactly | `:site` / `:deliver` |\n\n**Return frames (every frame, each `K[i]`).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| K1 | `inst(frame.pc−1)` in `frame.segment` is a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:return-site` |\n| K2 | `frame.rd = (code/rd inst(frame.pc−1))` | `:return-destination` |\n| K3 | every key of `frame.window` `< k(body(frame.pc))`; `frame.rd ∉ keys`; `(set (keys frame.window)) = (analysis/saved aₛ frame.pc frame.rd)` in that segment's analysis, reported as `:return-window-scope` / `:return-window-self` / `:return-window-extra` / `:return-window-missing` | as named |\n\n**Environments (every `E`: the act's and each return frame's).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | no binding key is reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`) | `:reserved-name` |\n| E2 | **`E` is the free environment under the lexical chain.** With `C = (set (mapcat params (chain (body pc))))` the lexically bound names at that pc (a return frame's chain is that of its own `pc`): (a) **every name of `C` is a key of `bindings`**, a nil binding being a present key (`bind-params` nil-fills, so a lexical name is never absent; an absent one would be nil-filled on lower and break the canonical round trip); (b) `(apply dissoc bindings C) = (apply dissoc (:free-env task) C)` as maps of `V`: a key outside `C` the free environment lacks, a free key `E` lacks, or a differing value, is a defect | `:free-env-mismatch` |\n\n**Task (once per body, before any frame; `frame/task-defect`).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| X1 | the root's `free-env` decodes to a map `{S V …}` with symbol keys, sorted and unique on the wire, none reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`, the same check `create-vm` runs on its `:env`, `vm/check-bindings!`, `vm.cljc:161-168`); it is the `:free-env` every E2 compares against | `:free-env-shape` / `:reserved-name` (path `[:free-env]`) |\n\n**Pending and contexts.**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured` and `:parked` require it absent | `:pending-presence` |\n| P2 | (`:wait`) the pending's reason is admitted by `inst(pc−1)` (act) or `inst(site)` (tail): `:stream-next → :next`; `:stream-put → :put`; `:ffi-call → :ffi, :ffi-request`; `:call → :next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response :install`; `:park` and `:current-continuation` admit none (frozen §4.1; `wire-of-kind`, `handoff.cljc:491-504`; ucf-revisions §8) | `:reason-mismatch` |\n| P3 | (`:captured`) the frame is an `act` and `inst(pc−1)` is `:current-continuation` | `:captured-site` |\n| P4 | (`:parked`) either `inst(pc−1)` is `:park` (an `act`), or `(:parked-key task) ∈ (:ffi-call-ids task)` and the frame is an `act` with `inst(pc−1) = :ffi-call` or a `tail` whose `inst(site)` is a tail `:call` (bookkeeping, evaluator §1.6) | `:parked-site` |\n\nRule E2 is the correction the review required (§4.4): a merged `E`\ncannot be split into free and lexical parts without the task's free\nenvironment, so the carrier carries it and the validator checks that\nevery `E` agrees with it off the lexical names. Nothing here reads a\nstack depth, a stack base or a `live` vector.\n\n### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n\n**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\nreview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\nhashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\npending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\ncomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\ncontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\njudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\nis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\nfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\nand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\nover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\nevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\nreconstructs.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n","numLines":470,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1762}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HUYRAYZpubbgatxtoyJV2h","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
